+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Options Considered | Rationale | Implications |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Use 2Q eviction policy | LRU (simple, fast adaptation), frequency-based with aging (good retention of popular clips, but adds counter maintenance), **2Q (protects reused sheets during scans)** | 2Q trades a moderate increase in bookkeeping complexity for protection of the working set (comparison sheets) during long sequential screenings. This aligns with the observed editing pattern: long sequential scans followed by repeated comparison of small working sets. The complexity is bounded to the preview worker and invisible to callers. | Implementation maintains two separate queues (A1 and Am) with size-based promotion logic. A1 is fixed at 1/4 cache size; this split must be validated against pilot workload patterns. |
+
+## Private Cache and Eviction Policy
 
 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction
+
+The cache uses a Two-Queue (2Q) eviction policy to protect comparison sheets during long sequential screening scans. The policy maintains two separate queues and transitions entries based on access patterns:
+
+- **A1 queue (first-use entries)**: Receives all new preview entries on first load. Sized at 1/4 of the total cache capacity (3 GiB). Eviction from A1 discards the entry.
+- **Am queue (active/reused entries)**: Receives entries accessed again after eviction from A1, and all entries reused within their A1 residence time. The remaining 3/4 of capacity (9 GiB) is available to both A1 and Am. Within Am, eviction uses LRU order.
+
+When a request hits A1, the entry is promoted to Am on that access. A1 misses that become Am entries go directly to Am. Cache size constraints are enforced before admitting any entry; A1 has a hard size limit to prevent a large screening scan from evicting all Am entries.
+
+This strategy resists one-off sequential scans (they fill A1 and evict from A1 without disturbing Am) while retaining frequently accessed comparison sheets in Am. The queue-size split and promotion threshold are implementation details invisible to callers.
